{
  "dom_id": "step-q214001",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A=\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta \\}.$ \n\n$$\nR=\\{(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\alpha ,\\beta ),(\\beta ,\\alpha ),(\\beta ,\\beta ),(\\beta ,\\delta ),(\\delta ,\\beta )\\}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\n$\\in R$ and \n{{field-2}}\n$\\in R,$ but \n{{field-3}}\n$\\notin R,$ the relation is\n{{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\n- Consider the pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ whose second component is $\\beta .$ It can be linked with the following pairs from $R$ whose first component is $\\beta :$ \n\n- Linking $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ with $(\\beta ,\\alpha )$ requires that $(\\alpha ,\\alpha )\\in R,$ and this pair is in $R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Linking $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ with $(\\beta ,\\delta )$ requires that $(\\alpha ,\\delta )\\in R.$ However, this pair is *not* in $R.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nWe can stop checking since we have found a chain of pairs that do not satisfy the transitivity condition. Therefore, we can conclude the following:\n\nSince $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R$ and $(\\beta ,\\delta )\\in R,$ but $(\\alpha ,\\delta )\\notin R,$ the relation is $\\text{not transitive}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
